---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// Dependency order (brief, 2026-11-02): json-rules → permissions → transitions → prisma-map → atlas → rules-builder → config → gloss.
const packages = [
  {
    name: "json-rules",
    line: "One rule that runs in memory, in Prisma and in SQL.",
    points: [
      "One rule AST compiles to an in-memory check, a Prisma query and a SQL WHERE clause for PostgreSQL or MySQL. Write the rule once and enforce it wherever it has to run.",
      "Rules are plain JSON. Store them, send them, let a tenant write one.",
      "Operators, paths and bindings are type-checked against your models.",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "permissions",
    line: "RBAC, ABAC and ReBAC in one engine, decided on your own records.",
    points: [
      "No separate tuple store to keep in sync. Access is decided on the rows you already have.",
      "Entitlements and delegates: a grant is checked again against whoever granted it, every time it is used. You can only hand on what you could do yourself.",
      "Rules are json-rules, down to a rule a tenant writes on one record.",
      "About a thousand lines of core.",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "transitions",
    line: "Declarative lifecycles on json-rules.",
    points: [
      "Guards a proposed state change and lists the transitions open right now.",
      "Workflow rules and permission rules are written in the same language.",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "prisma-map",
    line: "Your Prisma schema, readable at runtime.",
    points: [
      "Reads models, fields, relations, foreign-key direction and enums from a generated Prisma client.",
      "Lets rules and permissions walk your real data graph without reaching into Prisma's internals.",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "atlas",
    line: "A codebase map that can't drift.",
    points: [
      "Reads @atlas annotations and checks them against a concept registry your repo owns.",
      "Only states what is mechanically true, so CI can hold it.",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "rules-builder",
    line: "A headless rule builder that only offers what the engine can run.",
    points: [
      "A React hook that builds conditions against the fields and operators the engine supports. Bring your own components.",
    ],
    demo: "/rules-builder/",
  },
  {
    name: "config",
    line: "One shared toolchain, and the theme.",
    points: [
      "TypeScript, Biome and tsup presets, plus a version manifest every repo checks in CI.",
      "Ships the shared theme this site is drawn in.",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "gloss",
    line: "Comment-free source, with explanations on the side.",
    points: [
      "Moves explanation out of the code into a mirrored sidecar tree: src/foo.ts gets .gloss/src/foo.ts.md.",
      "You read the code, and open the sidecar only when you need the explanation.",
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Building blocks: stop rediscovering what you need to build · inixiative"
  description="A decade of backend lessons as open libraries and a SaaS template you own: json-rules, permissions, transitions, prisma-map, atlas, rules-builder, config and gloss."
  domain="building-blocks"
  current="building-blocks"
>
  <section class="hero anchor-target" id="primitives">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Building blocks</span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Stop rediscovering what you need to build.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      These are the answers I kept needing on every product: who can see a record, which state change
      is allowed, how one rule runs in memory, in Prisma and in SQL. Template is like shadcn, but for the
      whole SaaS backend: you start from it and you own the code. It is built from open libraries you can
      also use on their own, and six of them run in production at the company where I work.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">If your coding agent writes its own versions instead, <a class="text-link" href="/agentic/">Foundry</a> points it back at these.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section anchor-target" id="template">
    <div class="card" data-raised>
      <div class="card-head">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>template</span>
        <span class="tag" data-tone="progress">In progress</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="display-3">A SaaS backend where the hard parts are already built.</h2>
      <p>
        Authorization, multi-tenancy, field-level encryption, auditing, background jobs, webhooks and a
        generated type-safe client are working systems here, with tests.
      </p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><strong>Permissions over your real data</strong>Declarative rules over the data graph, checked by the permissions engine.</li>
        <li><strong>Routes write their own contract</strong>Add a route and controller; it registers itself, emits OpenAPI 3.1 and regenerates a typed SDK.</li>
        <li><strong>Responses checked against the spec</strong>A controller can only send the responses its route declares, and payloads are validated at runtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Errors as a system</strong>One error body everywhere, with guidance per status and request correlation.</li>
        <li><strong>Encryption with key rotation</strong>A field-level encryption service with a scheduled rotation job.</li>
        <li><strong>Legible to agents</strong>atlas annotations generate a codebase map that can't drift.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="meta">bun · hono · prisma 7 · react · turborepo · postgres</p>
      <div class="card-links">
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative/template">GitHub</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative/template/blob/main/COMPARISONS.md">Compared with managed services</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>The libraries</span>
      <h2>In dependency order.</h2>
      <p class="lede">json-rules sits under most of the stack. Each one is a package you can read and use on its own.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="chain" aria-label="Dependency order">
      {packages.map((p) => <li><a href={`#${p.name}`}>{p.name}</a></li>)}
    </ol>
    <ul class="packages">
      {packages.map((p) => (
        <li class="package" id={p.name}>
          <div class="package-name">
            <h3>{p.name}</h3>
            <div class="tags"><span class="tag" data-tone="open">MIT</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="package-body">
            <p class="line">{p.line}</p>
            <ul>{p.points.map((point) => <li>{point}</li>)}</ul>
            <div class="card-links">
              <a class="text-link" href={`https://github.com/inixiative/${p.name}`}>GitHub</a>
              <a class="text-link" href={`https://www.npmjs.com/package/@inixiative/${p.name}`}>npm</a>
              {p.demo && <a class="text-link" href={p.demo}>Live demo</a>}
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" data-compact>
    <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Paused</span>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card anchor-target" id="hivemind" data-plain>
        <div class="card-head"><h3>hivemind</h3><span class="tag">Paused</span></div>
        <p>Multi-agent coordination over MCP: a shared event log with role-scoped channels. Not recommended for use.</p>
        <div class="card-links"><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative/hivemind">GitHub</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card anchor-target" id="conditional-form" data-plain>
        <div class="card-head"><h3>conditional-form</h3><span class="tag">Paused</span></div>
        <p>Forms whose show, hide and validation logic is the same rule language the backend uses. Not recommended for use.</p>
        <div class="card-links"><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative/conditional-form">GitHub</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
